WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:05.000
A business needs $146,600 for equipment repair. How does a lender decide whether future daily sales can repay it?

00:05.000 --> 00:12.000
The operator creates one cohort of 750 simulated merchants. Each application will be evaluated twice.

00:12.000 --> 00:19.000
The Baseline experiment role keeps the reference policy. Both economic scenarios run within this submission.

00:19.000 --> 00:25.000
Review & run opens the campaign summary. Check the population, financing policy and output location before submitting.

00:25.000 --> 00:30.000
The local financial return passed. The desktop work shown next comes from the subsequent amended Power BI review.

00:30.000 --> 00:41.000
A supportable path includes an authorization or a referral for review. Funding requires authorization under the modeled policy.

00:41.000 --> 00:49.000
Tableau pairs each merchant’s decisions. A cell shows how the same applications were classified in the two scenarios.

00:49.000 --> 00:58.000
One merchant moved from approval as requested to an authorized counteroffer. The selected row identifies merchant 000703.

00:58.000 --> 01:05.000
For merchant 000703 the approved amount fell by $100. The other 34 merchants funded twice kept the same principal.

01:05.000 --> 01:14.000
A second case isolates repayment. Merchant 000462 received the same $146,600 advance for equipment repair in both scenarios.

01:14.000 --> 01:24.000
The remittance is a share of eligible sales, capped at the remaining balance. A day with no eligible sales collects zero.

01:24.000 --> 01:42.000
Follow the daily balances. They match through day 60. Baseline pays off on day 111. Stress ends day 120 with $3,743.44 open.

01:42.000 --> 01:52.000
Follow all 35 merchants funded in both. Twenty-eight paid off on the same day. One still owed money under stress at day 120.

01:52.000 --> 02:00.000
Try a new comparison. Reuse the saved population as a Challenger, change one policy setting, and compare the new results.
